verification_matrix — implementation plan
=========================================

The MUnit manifest is a useful static catalog, but it represents only
Scala-owned suites.  This plan standardizes its T relationship as `covers`.
PR 17 already defines the corresponding Isabelle mechanism: named `spec_test`
declarations are accumulated in `Theory_Data` and exported as structured
theory-test metadata.  The matrix must consume that metadata, not infer tests
from theory source text.  Procedural Python end-to-end tests need the same
discoverable relationship without pretending that discovery means execution.

goal
----

Assemble one deterministic static verification matrix from independent test
producers.  It answers which registered test verifies or covers each active
plan record, at which layer, and where that test is defined.  It never claims
that a test ran or passed.

relations
---------

`verifies`
  Links a registered test to an ACTIVE A or ACTIVE I record.

`covers`
  Links a registered test to a T requirement.  This replaces the MUnit API and
  manifest relation named `discharges`.

D and Q records cannot be test-link targets.  Their closure rules come from the
label schema and planning gate.

producer contract
-----------------

Every producer emits the same versioned JSON shape without executing test
bodies (the `schema` value carries the contract version):

    schema, producer, artifact, tests[]

Each test carries a stable producer-local identity, human name, layer, source
path and line, plus sorted `verifies` and `covers` ID lists.  Producer-specific
artifact hashes may detect a stale discovery catalog but are not execution
evidence.

Initial producers are:

- MUnit discovery: scala-unit, heap, and bridge;
- Isabelle theory metadata: ml-unit;
- planning-gate pytest discovery: tooling-unit; and
- the procedural Python producer from `plans/python_e2e`: e2e.

The shared layer registry gains `tooling-unit`.  Every declared layer must have
exactly one registered runner owner, even if several discovery sources feed it.

Isabelle producer boundary
--------------------------

PR 17 (https://github.com/edoput/pide-mcp/pull/17) is the authoritative
implementation of Isabelle theory-test registration.  A registered theory test
is a named `spec_test` record carrying its session, theory, source location and
digest, layer, and `verifies`/`covers` links.  `\<^assumption>` citations,
section headings, comments, and bare ML assertions are not registrations and
never create matrix coverage.

The planning gate consumes the generated Isabelle JSON manifest and adapts its
records to the common producer contract.  It validates the manifest producer
and schema, participation by every configured test session and every exported
`spec_test`-bearing theory, stable test identities, source paths and freshness
digests, layer-registry digest, unique links, and relation/claim compatibility.
A missing or stale manifest is a missing producer; discovery must fail closed
instead of falling back to source scanning.

Issue 20 (https://github.com/edoput/pide-mcp/issues/20) may replace PR 17's
YXML-to-JSON transport with direct JSON exports.  That is an exporter refactor
behind this boundary: it must preserve the same semantic fields and
completeness/freshness properties, and must not require a change to matrix
assembly.  In particular, each session aggregate must import every theory that
declares `spec_test`; omission is an error, not an empty result.  The Scala
MUnit runner remains independent of that transport change.

Before integration, PR 17's external relation name `discharges` must be renamed
to `covers` so the Isabelle and MUnit manifests share the terminology defined
by this plan.  The matrix does not retain a permanent compatibility alias for
the removed relation.

completion coverage
-------------------

- Every ACTIVE A/I record has at least one verifying test in every named layer.
- Every T record has at least one distinct covering test in every named layer.
- A test belongs to one layer; one identity cannot satisfy two required layers.
- MOOT and SUPERSEDED A/I records require no test links.
- Unknown, mistyped, duplicate, wrong-layer, and dangling relationships fail.
- A missing producer never becomes an implicit waiver.

legacy ratchet
--------------

Generate `plans/legacy_unlinked.csv` once after label normalization.  A row is
`plan,id,relation,missing_layer,baseline_revision`.  It describes a missing
verification relationship, including a separate row for each missing layer.

The matrix gate permits only this reviewed legacy debt.  Rows are removed as
coverage lands.  Adding a row is reported prominently and requires explicit
human review; a plan newly transitioning to done may not retain a row.

implementation sequence
-----------------------

1. Define typed producer and matrix records in `tools/planning_gate/matrix.py`.
2. Rename the MUnit `discharges` API/schema relation to `covers`; reject old and
   new relation names appearing together.
3. Land or integrate PR 17's `spec_test` exporter with its relation renamed to
   `covers`; Issue 20 may change its serialization without weakening the output
   contract.
4. Adapt the generated Isabelle manifest into ml-unit matrix records, preserving
   sessions, theory/test identities, exact source positions and freshness
   digests.  Never inspect theory source to manufacture registrations.
5. Add isolated manifest fixtures for missing sessions, stale sources, duplicate
   identities and links, wrong relations/layers, and a citation-only theory that
   contributes no coverage.
6. Add tooling-unit discovery for the planning package's pytest cases.
7. Merge producer output deterministically and validate it against normalized
   plan claims and the shared layer registry.
8. Generate and check the legacy CSV.  Separate generated current coverage
   reports from the reviewed debt file.
9. Keep matrix discovery independently callable.  The final `done` command
   builds both Isabelle test sessions and materializes their current manifest
   before composing discovery with execution.

non-goals
---------

- Recording test outcomes or durations.
- Judging the semantic adequacy of a test-to-claim link.
- Treating coverage percentages as completion.
- Reimplementing Isabelle theory-test registration or parsing `.thy` source as
  a substitute for PR 17's structured exports.
